From 47d64671b2203c2287d1ad50d51823743c261a00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 08:32:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] A build the preparer is told to trust must not print a
 warning

Reported from the preparer's PC, on python 3.13:

  tools\build_python_app.py:219: SyntaxWarning: invalid escape sequence '\s'
     * PLANS THE TEAM SHARES HAVE A PLACE: data\shared\workspaces\ , created at

Nothing is wrong with what it built. "\s" starts no escape python knows, so
python keeps the two characters as they are and the notice reads exactly as
intended; the 16 files of dist/PM_APP_py are byte-for-byte identical before
and after this change, sha256 compared.

It still had to go, for two reasons. It appears in the middle of a build the
runbook tells the preparer to trust, and a warning there stops a hand that
should be moving. And python has been escalating this since 3.6 - a
DeprecationWarning then, a SyntaxWarning from 3.12, an error in some later
version - so this is a build that will one day refuse to run.

Why it reached them and not me is the part worth naming: this interpreter is
3.11, where the warning is a DeprecationWarning and hidden by default. That
is the third finding in a row that could not be seen from here - after a
windows-only branch and a linux-only browser path - so the fix is a check,
not a habit. test_layers.py now compiles every .py under src/ and tools/ and
fails on any warning at all, whatever python is running it. Proven by
reintroducing one: caught, named with file and line.

The spelling changed; no value did. One notice in build_python_app.py and six
regex literals in test_charts.py now double the backslash (\\s, \\d, \\/),
which is how python spells what it was already doing. All 428 string
constants across the two files compare equal to their old values.

